# Canonical Program OS Cursor Rules

This repo powers Canonical Program OS.

Core purpose:
Turn scattered project files, curriculum materials, AI outputs, workflows, and institutional memory into one coherent operating system for educators, builders, and operators.

Primary rails:
- AYA/CTS: institutional and classroom-facing materials
- PRISM: private framework, facilitator logic, owner-only program architecture
- CANONICAL: system infrastructure, inbox parsing, generation pipelines, connector logic

Rules:
- Do not expose PRISM private content to public/demo users.
- Do not mix PRISM private framework language into AYA/CTS student-facing materials.
- Do not unlock owner/admin mode by query string alone.
- Owner/Admin mode requires verified auth and authorization.
- Do not fake live connector functionality.
- Label features truthfully as demo, dry-run, owner available, live enabled, declared only, or not implemented.
- Prefer small patches over broad refactors.
- Audit before implementation.
- One fix per step unless explicitly instructed otherwise.
- Never commit, publish, move files, change DNS, or run live connector writes unless explicitly instructed.

## PRISM owner/admin visibility rule

PRISM private data is not public data.

Verified owner/admin users should be able to access full PRISM program data, facilitator logic, private framework notes, source indexes, and owner-only program architecture inside Owner/Admin mode.

Public, demo, and authenticated non-owner users must only see demo-safe summaries.

Do not place private PRISM data in publicly bundled frontend content if it must remain truly private. If PRISM content is sensitive, load it through an authenticated Base44 function/entity path that verifies owner/admin access before returning it.

UI hiding is not security. Query-string mode is not security. Owner/admin authorization must happen before private PRISM data is returned or rendered.

Never expose secrets, connector tokens, raw credentials, or live write keys in frontend code, owner mode, demo mode, logs, or diagnostics.
For multi-agent workflow, governance, approval gates, and implementation flow, follow docs/CANONICAL_Agent_Council_Flow_v0_1.md.